#! files=10000 flat=20000 laps=25
# Tier B: what the process holds, rather than how long anything takes.
#
# Five questions, each a `mem` (which drops the layout cache and has the allocator hand back what it can): the
# floor with a window on a small folder; ten thousand rows listed; twenty thousand in a flat view;
# whether leaving gives all of that back; and whether doing it again and again keeps anything — the
# two soaks, where a leak of a few kilobytes a lap is invisible in one and unmissable in twenty-five.
#
# The Rust allocator (the core) and the process heap (the C++ side and the system DLLs) are the numbers to read,
# each blind to the other, so a soak is held to both. The Rust number repeats to the kilobyte between runs,
# which is why its budget is the tighter. The working set here is the harness's,
# under the software rasteriser; the real executable's is `startup.memory.*` in Tier C.
# The `since` budgets are on growth, not size, so they hold on any machine; `compare` judges the sizes.

bench-fixture 10000 0 Big
bench-fixture 20000 200 Deep
flat-cap 100000
mem idle

go Big
assert-count 10000
mem listing-10k
up

go Deep
flat on
assert-count 20000
mem flat-20k
flat off
up
mem released

repeat 25 go Big; up
mem soak-navigate
assert-mem allocator under 1 since released
assert-mem heap under 8 since released

repeat 15 newtab Big; closetab
assert-tabs 1
mem soak-tabs
assert-mem allocator under 1 since soak-navigate
assert-mem heap under 8 since soak-navigate
assert-not-launched
